    "title": "How Does University of Maryland Baltimore County Rank Among America's Best Colleges?",
    "text": "University of Maryland Baltimore County is a public institution that was founded in 1966. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, University of Maryland Baltimore County is ranked No. #127 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #19 in Most Innovative Schools. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 10,767 (fall 2024), its setting is suburban, and the campus size is 530 acres. The student-faculty ratio at University of Maryland Baltimore County is 17:1, and it utilizes a 4-1-4-based academic calendar. University of Maryland Baltimore County accepts the Common Application. The school's in-state tuition and fees are $13,539; out-of-state tuition and fees are $31,225. Forty-nine percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $19,319. The four-year graduation rate is 48%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $55,137.University of Maryland Baltimore County is a public institution that was founded in 1966. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, University of Maryland Baltimore County is ranked No. #127 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #19 in Most Innovative Schools. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 10,767 (fall 2024), its setting is suburban, and the campus size is 530 acres. The student-faculty ratio at University of Maryland Baltimore County is 17:1, and it utilizes a 4-1-4-based academic calendar. University of Maryland Baltimore County accepts the Common Application. The school's in-state tuition and fees are $13,539; out-of-state tuition and fees are $31,225. Forty-nine percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $19,319. The four-year graduation rate is 48%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $55,137.",
